From d170c3536b42987987278b44392091c17d803c98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:29:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PRIORITY: an accepted door render becomes the kitchen's color
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Owner rule: any approved render becomes the kitchen's color.

Accepting a door-rendering run only ever inserted a version row and an 'accepted'
event. Nothing reached door_styles and the Designer had no way to read an accepted
run, so a kitchen never saw an approved render and kept drawing the flat catalog
hex — Shaker Honey (SH, Madison) rendered #c8a040 instead of the accepted light
tan birch that scored 10.0.

door_styles now carries its latest accepted run: renderHex, the approved door
image, the approved version id, and when it was accepted.

The writeback lives in recordDecision, the one function every acceptance,
revocation, and revert already goes through, so there is no second path to keep in
sync:

  - accept  -> the run's renderHex and approved image are written;
  - revoke  -> they are cleared, and the style's saved color takes over;
  - revert  -> they follow the version it reverted to, or clear if none is left.

A writeback failure is logged and never fails the approval itself: the version and
its event are already stored.

doorStyles.acceptedAppearance is a PUBLIC read of one style's (or a list's)
accepted appearance. Public because a shopper's Designer reads it too, and it is
what the kitchen renders from. It is not cached beyond the normal query, so
approving a run reaches every kitchen on the next read with no manual step. Only a
row with an approvedVersionId counts as approved, so a renderHex typed into the
style editor without accepting a run never drives a kitchen. No database returns
"nothing approved" rather than throwing.

Columns are nullable and added by docs/sql/0245_door_style_accepted_run.tidb.sql.
That file only adds columns; it inserts, updates, and deletes nothing.
